## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced architectural decision-making under production constraints; evaluates understanding of efficient attention patterns, tokenization strategies, and deployment optimization techniques.

## Key Concepts

- **Attention strategies**: Full vs sparse approaches, global tokens for maintaining long-range dependencies
- **Positional encodings**: Rotary vs learned embeddings for effective length extrapolation
- **Tokenization**: BPE fundamentals, domain adaptation techniques, code-switching handling methods
- **Deployment**: Quantization strategies, paged caching implementations, asynchronous batching approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: windowed attention with global tokens
function hybridAttention(tokens, globalTokens, windowSize) {
  const localAttention = computeWindowedAttention(tokens, windowSize);
  const globalAttention = computeGlobalAttention(tokens, globalTokens);
  return combineAttentions(localAttention, globalAttention);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of attention scaling, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs in production-grade transformers.

## Key Concepts
- Block-local attention and memory scaling
- Attention masking within a window
- Q/K/V reshaping for block processing
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs latency vs memory

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: local attention in blocks
function localAttention(Q, K, V, block=256, window=128){
  // Q,K,V: [B, T, D]
  const B = Q.length, T = Q[0].length, D = Q[0][0].length;
  let O = Array.from({length:B}, () => Array.from({length:T}, () => new Array(D).fill(0)));
  for (let b=0; b<B; b++){
    for (let s=0; s<T; s+=block){
      const end = Math.min(T, s+block);
      for (let t=s; t<end; t++){
        const wStart = Math.max(0, t-window);
        const wEnd = Math.min(T, t+window+1);
        // compute attention over [wStart, wEnd)
        // A = softmax((Q[t]·K[w].T)/sqrt(D))
        // O[b][t] = sum_{w=wStart}^{wEnd-1} A[w]·V[w]
      }
    }
  }
  return O;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does changing window size affect long-range dependency capture and memory?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect degradation when switching from global to local attention?

## Why This Is Asked

To assess the ability to design policy-aware, scalable agent orchestration that handles multi-tenant data isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration with AutoGen planning
- Private APIs and data catalog integration
- Policy enforcement and tenant isolation
- End-to-end traceability, auditing, and failure handling
- Resilience patterns: retries, backoff, circuit breakers

## Code Example

```javascript
async function plan(request) {
  const { userId, tenantId, dataScope } = request;

  const policyResult = await policyEngine.check({ tenantId, dataScope });
  if (!policyResult.allowed) {
    throw new Error('Access denied: policy violation');
  }

  const catalogResult = await dataCatalog.query({ tenantId, dataScope });
  const apiResult = await privateApi.fetch({ tenantId, dataScope });

  await auditLogger.log({
    tenantId,
    userId,
    steps: [policyResult, catalogResult, apiResult],
    traceId: uuid()
  });

  return { data: apiResult, traceId: traceId };
}
```

This flow ensures tenant-scoped policies are enforced at entry, each component call is traced, and failures are handled with compensating transactions or graceful degradation.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of attention math, dimensions, scaling, and how tokenization affects token-to-token alignment in real models.

## Key Concepts

- Dot-product attention math and scaling (√d_k)
- Softmax over keys for each query
- Tokenization impact on alignment
- Practical pitfalls in batching

## Code Example

```javascript
function simpleAttention(q, k, v) {
  // q: [d], k: [n, d], v: [n, d]
  const scores = k.map(row => dot(q, row) / Math.sqrt(d));
  const weights = softmax(scores);
  return weights.reduce((acc, w, idx) => acc + w * v[idx], 0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests focus on practical evaluation rather than high-level theory. This question probes ability to design simple actionable metrics, masking rules, and small-scale experiments, all in a beginner-friendly way that translates to real-world translation pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- Boundary-aware attention
- Subword tokenization
- Word-level alignment
- Simple masking strategies
- Small datasets and quick experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal example to compute anchor-based metric
function computeAnchorMetric(attn, starts) {
  // attn: 2D array [t_len][s_len]
  // starts: Set of source indices that are word starts
  let score = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t < attn.length; t++) {
    let sumAnchor = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < attn[t].length; s++) {
      if (starts.has(s)) sumAnchor += attn[t][s];
    }
    score += sumAnchor;
  }
  return attn.length ? score / attn.length : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multilingual or morphologically rich languages?
- How would you validate with human annotations?

## Why This Question Matters
Provides a practical, beginner-friendly approach to assessing grounding, code fidelity, and relevance for a developer assistant across private documentation corpora with versioning support.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding responses to exact doc version and section
- Detection of code block hallucinations
- Metrics: precision@1, grounding coverage, and citation drift

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal runner skeleton
const prompts = loadPrompts('prompts-50.json');
prompts.forEach(p => {
  const ans = model.ask(p);
  const grounded = verifyGrounding(ans, p.docVersion, p.section);
  const hallucinated = detectHallucinations(ans, p.docVersion);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    prompt: p.question,
    grounded,
    hallucinated,
    precisionAt1: computePrecision(ans, p.expectedEndpoints)
  }));
});
```
